WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.210
- Good afternoon, everyone.
My name is Priya Natarajan.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.520
I'm the current director

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:17.003
of The Franke Program in Science
and the Humanities at Yale.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.640
It is with deep sadness
that I would like to share

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.830
with you all the terrible news

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.530
of the passing of Richard Franke.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:30.420
Rich was one of the founding
duo along with Barbara Franke,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.760
who has kindly joined us today,

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.210
of this flagship
interdisciplinary program,

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:39.270
The Franke Program in Science
and the Humanities at Yale.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:43.380
Today we mourn the passing
of a family member,

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.380
a kind, generous, and wise
elder who guided many of us.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.690
To me personally, he was
an inveterate cheerleader,

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:56.290
keeping track of my
research work in science,

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.190
in astrophysics, sending
congratulatory notes

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.730
whenever he read about my work.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.190
We would meet up over coffee,
and he would pepper me

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:07.570
with really hard science questions.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:10.620
You know, he really was a space nerd

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.940
and had an incredibly curious mind

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.960
and was so widely read and knowledgeable

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.860
that it was just incredible how
he spanned all these worlds,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.660
academia, the creative
arts, the business world,

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.220
politics, and philanthropy.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.950
Both he and Barbara
remained strong supporters

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.820
of all our activities,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.660
and I have very fond pre-pandemic memories

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.000
of all of us, the Franke family

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.700
along with the young
interdisciplinary scholars,

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.750
our undergrads, grads, and postdocs

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:42.400
getting together over dinner,

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.820
discussing bold new ideas
to explore and study.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.140
He paid attention to all the talks,

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.210
asked incisive questions,
and felt very proud

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:56.080
of the young people whose
intellectual adventures

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.610
were not limited by academic silos

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.263
or disciplinary boundaries.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:03.983
I'm at a loss for words.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.240
Let me quote from a beautiful poem

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:10.293
by the Lebanese American
poet Khalil Gibran,

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:14.880
in which this, the book
from "The Prophet,"

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.210
in which it's a conversation

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:20.597
between the prophet and a human being.

00:02:20.597 --> 00:02:22.750
"You would know the secret of death,

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.100
but how shall you find it

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.040
unless you seek it in the heart of life?

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.530
The owl whose night-bound
eyes are blind unto the day

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.220
cannot unveil the mystery of light.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.810
If you would indeed behold
the spirit of death,

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:39.540
open your heart wide
unto the body of life,

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.730
for life and death are one,

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.797
even as the river and the sea are one."

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.270
So let's have a moment
of silence in his memory

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:53.020
to remember him as he
was, deeply empathetic

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:57.350
and a beautiful human being,
always smiling, energetic,

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:01.270
and brimming with
curiosity to forever learn.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:03.640
Barbara, we mourn with you

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.890
and the rest of your family today,

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.050
and we wish you strength and courage

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.630
at this extremely difficult time.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:13.743
You are both an inspiration to us all.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.060
So let's have a moment of silence

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.003
before we start today's program.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.650
Let's begin by recognizing
our benefactors,

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.600
Mr. and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.733
acknowledging the sad loss
of Mr. Franke last Friday.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:08.660
Today's talk is one that we
have all been looking forward to

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.200
with great excitement,
and it is with great pride

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.510
and pleasure that I introduce our speaker,

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:19.160
one of our own Franke Fellows

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:22.040
who started this wonderful project,

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.950
cross-disciplinary project,

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:26.980
and despite many personal challenges,

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:30.530
health challenges has emerged victorious,

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:35.230
enthusiastic, and super-productive
in terms of his work.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:38.650
So the talk today is by Diego Ellis Soto,

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:41.170
and the title is "Collective Pulse:

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.170
Uncovering the Hidden Lives of Animals

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:47.930
through Music Theory and
Artificial Intelligence."

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.120
I can tell you all

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.540
that you're in for a treat this afternoon.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:55.580
First let me remind you that
we are recording this event,

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:57.040
and therefore all participants

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.470
will have to have their videos muted

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.600
for the duration of the talk.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.600
Questions may be submitted at
any time in the Q&amp;A feature.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:11.870
So let me introduce our young
and brilliant scholar, Diego.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.760
Diego is an Uruguayan biologist

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.790
studying how biodiversity across the world

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:21.620
responds to future, current
and future climatic conditions

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.970
in a rapidly warming world,

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:28.460
work, as you all know, is
extremely urgent and timely.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.270
He tests ecological theory

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.100
using cutting-edge technologies,

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.800
animal GPS collar data, drone imagery,

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.480
citizen science data, satellite imagery,

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:41.790
statistical modeling for
applied conservation outcomes.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.790
He's particularly
interested in understanding

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:45.990
how, why, when, and where species move

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:49.350
at local, regional, and global scales.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.720
This is of course critical
in order to predict

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.620
the ecological consequences
of increased human activities

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.740
and changing climates on biodiversity.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.870
While the bulk of his PhD work involved

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:04.820
sitting between a computer
screen and a desk chair coding,

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:07.190
Diego has performed
field work and research

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.960
on the ecology of giant tortoises

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.460
in Galapagos Islands
for the past nine years

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.490
and is passionate about
scientific communication

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:18.910
as well as his research work.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:20.930
He joined Yale in 2018

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:23.470
and is currently a
fourth-year doctoral student

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:26.780
in the Department of Ecology
and Evolutionary Biology.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:29.480
Besides being a Franke Fellow,

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:32.250
one that we are extremely proud of,

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:35.900
Diego is also an early career
Rufford conservation explorer,

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:38.590
a Yale Global Food Fellow,

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:41.050
a Law, Ethics &amp; Animal Program Fellow

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:42.570
at the Yale Law School,

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:46.690
and an Environmental Humanities
student grant awardee.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:51.690
He exemplifies the best of
what Rich Franke had in mind

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:54.580
for a truly interdisciplinary scholar

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:57.290
working at the cutting edge,

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.240
using cutting-edge technologies

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:03.550
to answer some really old,
profound, deep questions.

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.370
He obtained his undergraduate education

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.880
in environmental sciences
from the University of Trier

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.340
and his graduate studies
in biological sciences

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.280
at the University of Konstanz.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:13.760
Shortly afterward, he worked

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.930
at the Max Planck Institute
for Animal Behavior,

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.310
leveraging large amounts
of biological data

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:20.820
for hundreds of species across the world.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:24.380
During this time, this is the
most enviable part of his CV,

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:27.240
he lived in the Galapagos
Islands for over a year,

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.810
working with the giant tortoises

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:32.120
and spent several months
in the Ecuadorian Amazon

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:34.760
working with poison frogs.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.610
He worked as a DJ throughout
his time in Germany

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:39.880
and performs music at the interface

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:42.363
of natural sounds and electronic music.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:48.210
I mean, I think his resume
is just impossibly filled

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.790
with sort of these talented things

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:54.380
that he has productively engaged in.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:56.460
I don't know how he has
time to do all of this

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:59.110
given how young he is, but there you go.

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:01.720
He's also very passionate
about scientific communication,

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:05.100
and of course his work, which
as you will see in a moment,

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:07.260
just is at the frontier.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:09.570
It has been featured in
dozens of news outlets

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:12.880
including "BBC Nature"
documentaries, in "New York Times,"

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:14.787
Spanish newspapers, "ABC News,"

00:08:14.787 --> 00:08:17.340
"Yahoo News," and so on and so forth.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:20.590
In addition, his most recent work research

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:23.410
is the impacts of altered human mobility

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:27.410
during COVID-19 pandemic on
wildlife across the world

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:29.460
and has been endorsed
by the United Nations.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:31.390
So COVID, as you all know,

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:34.630
the global pandemic not
only impacted us humans,

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.430
but the entire natural world.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:38.973
It was traumatic for the
entire natural world.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:44.150
Diego aspires to bring the humanities

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:45.750
and the natural sciences together,

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:48.400
both at work and in his spare time.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:53.010
He performs in electronic music
festivals across the globe,

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:54.950
and he's increasingly interested

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:58.450
in combining electronic
music with soundscape ecology

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:00.690
using open-source technology.

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.550
We are also delighted today
to have Matthew Suttor,

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:05.800
professor in the practice of sound design

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:06.980
at Yale School of Drama,

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:10.240
who will be joining Diego
today in his presentation.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.000
Professor Suttor has been a
leading part of this project

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.810
alongside Diego since the beginning.

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:18.880
We're particularly grateful
for his continued support

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.150
through personally
difficult times for Diego.

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:24.340
New Zealand-born composer Matthew Suttor

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:27.440
has taught at Yale University since 1999,

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:28.950
first in the Department of Music

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:32.780
and since 2002 at the David
Geffen School of Drama.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:34.200
He's also affiliate faculty

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:37.450
at the Center for Collaborative
Arts and Media at Yale.

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:41.320
Often combining music
technology with acoustic forces,

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:44.470
Matthew has composed operas,
dance works, and music

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.860
for all kinds of theatrical productions,

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.180
chamber music, and sacred
sound installations,

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:52.440
scores for television, and so on.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:55.920
His work in opera and dance
includes "Don Juan in Prague"

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:58.450
in collaboration with the
director David Chambers

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:00.463
for the Bard SummerScape Festival,

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:05.280
which was revised for the
Mozart Prague Festival,

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:07.683
the Guggenheim Works and Process series,

00:10:10.075 --> 00:10:12.023
and the BAM Next Wave Festival.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:18.300
He composed Zoomslide's
television series "Real Pasifik"

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:21.387
and Anna Marbrook's documentary
feature film "Loimata."

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:25.030
He has been involved with
Yale Rep Theatre productions

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:28.147
that include "The Intelligent
Design of Jenny Chow,"

00:10:28.147 --> 00:10:30.417
"All's Well That Ends
Well," "The Winter's Tale,"

00:10:30.417 --> 00:10:32.537
"Arcadia," and "An Enemy of the People."

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:36.350
He's extremely distinguished,
a Fulbright Scholar,

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.480
and he received a doctorate in composition

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:41.440
from Columbia University.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.630
I could spend the entire hour telling you

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:45.540
about the wonderful accomplishments

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:49.190
of our two scholars today,
but without further ado,

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:51.740
let me turn it over to Diego.

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:53.820
Diego and Matthew, thank you so much

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:56.793
for speaking to us today
and sharing your ideas.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:01.420
- Thank you so much, Priya,
for this kind introduction.

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:04.450
Thank you, Ms. Franke, for letting us

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:06.300
dream big with the support.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.550
So none of this would have been possible

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:10.820
without this initial support
from the Franke project,

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:15.190
and I hope to show how an
initial support towards students

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:17.300
can snowball into a larger project

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:19.260
that is "Collective Pulse" now.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:21.003
So thank you for believing in us.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:25.920
Today I'm very excited to
present that joint work.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.750
I'm extremely flattered
to be giving this talk.

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:30.320
It's a professional highlight for me.

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:32.540
Thank you for the opportunity.

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:35.860
This is a large team effort in
a truly collaborative manner,

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:38.290
as hopefully you will see
in the last acknowledgement

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:41.270
of my slide, involving people on farms,

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:44.880
local hospitals, engineers,
music, musicians,

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.473
composers, ecologists, conservationists.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:50.890
It's been truly a pleasure and a privilege

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:53.610
to be able to work with every
single one of these people

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:56.860
and particularly Matthew for
the patience, the mentorship,

00:11:56.860 --> 00:12:00.040
and the expertise and just the creativity

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:01.790
to dream big and take this forward.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:05.060
Today we'll be talking about new ways

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:06.650
of exploring the natural world

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:08.460
and looking at the hidden lives of animals

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:11.300
through a bird's-eye view, literally,

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:13.890
using new emergent
technologies that let us

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:17.790
capture the voyages of animals
and how they sound like

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:20.640
and how they look like and
the danger of what happens

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.300
if we lose this incredible beauty

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.660
in the natural world by human activities.

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:27.040
And I would like to introduce

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:29.540
the main characters of today's band.

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:32.340
We have flying pigeons on a piano.

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:34.860
We have homing pigeons
on a different piano.

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:37.420
We have stickleback fish on the drums.

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:40.330
And we have eight termites
that decided to make a mixture

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:42.373
of electronic music and classic guitar.

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:49.520
So the beauty of the natural
world knows no limit.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.880
Many of us probably have
seen these incredibly flocks

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.590
of tens of thousands of birds
flying at once in unison.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:58.930
And this looks incredibly harmonic,

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:01.090
and people have wondered for a long time,

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:02.843
how do these rules go together?

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:04.790
Do they have telepathy?

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:08.590
How do tens of thousands of
animals organize in unison?

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:10.080
And to many people like Matthew,

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:11.620
this probably looks like music,

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:13.870
and he will expand a
little bit more on this.

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:17.123
At the same time, how I started,

00:13:18.090 --> 00:13:19.900
this is me on the left next to my brother.

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:22.160
I'm the one with the
glasses as a five-year-old.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:25.720
I guess I've always liked
animals and biology.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:29.110
And on the right side, I
sort of always liked music.

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:31.500
I'm the little one watching
my brother jealously

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:36.240
playing the flute while my dad
is looking at musical sheets.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:37.780
But I always played my cards right.

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:40.470
This was outside an airport
where we asked adults

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:44.060
for money to then buy candy
and some dinosaur toys.

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:47.340
So I think I've always
had a passion for music,

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:49.510
getting a lot of different people together

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:52.450
to get the most out of a
project as a collective

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:54.820
and inspiration of the natural world.

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:56.650
I actually started in the humanities.

00:13:56.650 --> 00:13:59.030
I started as a journalist and then quit

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:00.870
and went to Germany to study biology.

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:03.290
Little did I know that
it would change my life.

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:07.470
Fast-forward 25 years, I
now study animal migrations,

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:10.500
particularly across the
world, and how they move

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:14.250
and how biodiversity is
moving under global warming.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:15.920
Here we can see a picture of the globe

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:19.390
where every single pink
line is a individual animal

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:21.940
tracked with a little GPS collar

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:25.010
that allows us to monitor
the voyages of these animals.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:26.620
And we can see almost that animals

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:30.250
are almost like veins or
pulses across the world.

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:34.620
As they are following snowmelt
in the Northern Hemisphere,

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:37.270
they're going up, and as snow comes back,

00:14:37.270 --> 00:14:40.040
animals are going down into
the Southern Hemisphere,

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:42.390
almost like a collective
pulse through the planet.

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:44.260
And this is something that
I've been investigating

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:46.023
in my PhD for a very long time.

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:52.470
And in my laboratory at Yale
University led by Walter Jetz,

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:54.920
we're also very interested
in creating tools

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:59.820
that allow the public to
visualize these voyages of animals

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:01.530
and represent the hidden lives of animals,

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:05.550
bringing them closer to the
public, such as Animal Lives.

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:06.810
But also during the last years,

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:08.940
I've been focusing more
on music production

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:11.127
using a variety of different softwares.

00:15:12.550 --> 00:15:16.780
On my PhD, I use a lot of
different technological advances

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:19.200
to improve the way that we can monitor

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:22.220
and document biodiversity
and how it's changing.

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:25.080
For example, on the right side,
we have over 900 satellites

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:28.030
measuring environmental
conditions 24 hours a day

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:29.970
that allows to measure
the pulse of the planet

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.883
in terms of vegetation
greenness, as seen on the left.

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:37.040
And this is incredibly important
and increasingly important

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:39.840
because we're currently in
the sixth mass extinction,

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.840
and this is a human man-made catastrophe

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:45.830
with human activities driving
several species to extinction.

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:48.260
And what's even worse
is there's many species

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:49.750
we don't even know about,

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:53.300
and we are losing not just
important ecosystem services

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:56.440
but also the incredible beauty of nature,

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:59.040
their sounds, the way they look like,

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:01.100
and it's really important to document this

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:03.400
and better understand
the change through time.

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:06.960
This is, for example, an
article that highlights

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:09.160
some scientific communication
of my research lab,

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:12.070
where through maps we're able to visualize

00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:14.260
biodiversity closer to humans.

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:16.750
And actually because
of this Franke project,

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:19.440
I've started to think
maps are a really, really

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:22.020
incredible way of communicating narratives

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:23.800
and scientific stories to humans.

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:25.663
But music can be the same, too.

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:30.970
And this is something that we
are increasingly able to do.

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:33.840
We can document human
activities through time,

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:35.210
and we're able to understand

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:38.200
the impacts of biology on these changes.

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:40.920
For example, we can see how urbanization

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:43.860
and human activities
are changing ecosystems

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.380
but also what ecosystems sound like.

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:49.590
And by here, I wanna say
that this is by no way

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:51.120
something that we have invented here.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:53.930
There has been many people
that have pioneered this work,

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:55.650
and I wanna highlight two of them,

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:57.500
namely Benjamin Patterson,

00:16:57.500 --> 00:17:01.563
who was an incredible
artist and was composed,

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:04.640
was employed as a classic bassist,

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:08.360
but he was denied employment
because of his race,

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:11.570
and he recently died 2016.

00:17:11.570 --> 00:17:13.490
But to my knowledge,
he was the first person

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:16.980
exploring how to make music with ants.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:20.840
And what Mr. Patterson
did, he put a music sheet

00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:23.590
on the ground and tossed ants on it

00:17:23.590 --> 00:17:27.000
and followed their movements
by drawing on a pen.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:28.600
And here you can see Mr. Patterson

00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:32.830
trying to make musical
sheet out of data of ants.

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:36.100
And he later has mentioned
that while this is not music

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:38.963
or musical, it's a new
method of making music.

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:42.230
I also want to acknowledge work

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:44.630
by Bernie Krause, a soundscape ecologist,

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:48.340
and he created the soundtrack
of "Apocalypse Now."

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:50.820
And on the left, we can see Mr. Krause

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:53.480
deploying some sensors
to look, some microphones

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:55.880
to look at habitat change in ecosystems.

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:58.100
And on the right, we can see Mr. Krause

00:17:58.100 --> 00:17:59.860
with one of the first Moog synthesizers

00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:03.360
such as Matthew has in
the back to make music.

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:05.187
And Mr. Krause also said,

00:18:05.187 --> 00:18:07.370
"A picture is worth a thousand words,

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:09.720
but a soundscape is worth
a thousand pictures."

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:13.510
And this is actually incredibly important.

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:17.520
And Mr. Krause has recently
wrote an incredible book

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:20.290
called "The Great Animal Opera"
with interesting insights

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:22.370
as of how humans learned how to dance

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:23.960
based on the natural world,

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:25.990
and his concept of the animal opera

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:29.020
was covered by the Sydney Biennale,

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:30.570
and what you can see,
there are sound waves

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:32.680
of the natural world
on the orchestra house

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:33.740
in the background.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:35.610
So there's increasingly appreciation

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:37.760
also in the larger public around the work

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:40.953
of documenting soundscapes
and documenting biodiversity.

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:45.830
And this is incredibly
important also as a biologist,

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:47.910
wearing my global change biology hat,

00:18:47.910 --> 00:18:51.460
because Mr. Krause recording from the '60s

00:18:51.460 --> 00:18:53.837
allows to document not just how sounds

00:18:53.837 --> 00:18:55.490
and ecosystems has changed,

00:18:55.490 --> 00:18:58.350
but whether species are still there or not

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:00.900
and why this might be and how fragile

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:03.600
soundscapes might be
due to human activities.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:05.730
So it's an incredibly powerful tool

00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:07.140
of scientific communication

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:08.810
but also to understand the natural world

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:10.310
from a scientific perspective.

00:19:14.150 --> 00:19:18.070
And what is new here in our project

00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:22.640
is that we are trying
to document and leverage

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:26.070
these new, incredible technologies
that are available to us

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:28.750
as scientists, namely
animal tracking technologies

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:31.280
that let us put up
satellite tech on an animal

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:33.210
and follow it its entire lifetime,

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:34.640
computer vision that allow us

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:37.170
to track hundreds of
animals simultaneously,

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:39.590
as we can see in the
middle, termites or fish,

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:42.550
up to the right side where
we have smartphone apps

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:45.070
that have machine learning algorithms

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:48.510
that allow us to identify any
bird calling in the real time.

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:53.170
You're going to see a
chickadee, I think, arrive

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:56.490
and being recognized almost
instantly by these algorithms.

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:59.100
So we're able to leverage
this incredibly amount

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:03.090
of biodiversity data for
conservation purposes, ecology,

00:20:03.090 --> 00:20:06.350
but also to bring this
incredibly fragility

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:09.050
of the natural world and incredible beauty

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:10.193
closer to the public.

00:20:11.730 --> 00:20:13.430
And we are not alone here.

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:14.610
As I said, there's many people

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:15.780
have thought deeply about this.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:19.580
And Aristotle mentioned
even in his book "De Anima"

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:23.090
written 2,000 years ago
that every kind of animal

00:20:23.090 --> 00:20:25.000
should be studied and revealed

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:27.170
as it has something incredibly beautiful

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:28.243
in the natural world.

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:32.120
Well think, fast-forward 2,000 years,

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:33.970
now we have really good, powerful AI

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:37.760
that allows us to track a
lot of different objects,

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:39.790
humans, and species in incredible detail,

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:41.910
and we're able to leverage this for tech,

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:44.130
for conservation, and for good.

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:47.840
As shown here in 2015 by Rosenthal et al.,

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:50.310
this is a Facebook social network of fish.

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:52.620
Here we can see a number of fish,

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:56.040
and the graphs and the nodes between them

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:58.300
is the relationship that
each of these fish has

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:00.500
to their neighbor and
their local interactions.

00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:03.000
So we can not just
uncover the hidden lives

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:05.410
of single animals, but
what's even more fascinating

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:07.223
is their collective behavior.

00:21:08.794 --> 00:21:11.540
And incredibly research
in collective behavior

00:21:11.540 --> 00:21:15.430
has shown that actually
very simple rules in nature

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:17.740
determine very complex patterns.

00:21:17.740 --> 00:21:20.870
As seen here, there's three
different collective states

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:22.320
that animals could be in,

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:24.700
and these are largely determined by,

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:27.210
animals don't wanna be
too far away in groups.

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:29.210
They don't wanna be too close together,

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:31.340
and they wanna move at a similar speed.

00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:33.470
And we could think of these
three different states

00:21:33.470 --> 00:21:36.240
as being almost universal in nature.

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:40.390
Swarming can occur in
mosquitoes, it can occur in fish.

00:21:40.390 --> 00:21:43.490
Polarization, when
there's almost a harmonic,

00:21:43.490 --> 00:21:45.370
very directional movement.

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:48.100
It's almost like a V formation in fish

00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:50.710
or a V formation in flying fish.

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:52.940
And milling can occur in fish or in,

00:21:52.940 --> 00:21:56.550
if any of you ever wants
to Google reindeer cyclone,

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:58.170
it's when reindeers move in a circle,

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.140
very fun video to watch.

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:01.940
But what on earth has this to do

00:22:01.940 --> 00:22:04.220
with music theory and music?

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:06.920
Well, this is when I had the
pleasure of meeting Matthew

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:09.410
right before the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:11.110
and we started talking about these things,

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:13.360
and it has been an absolute pleasure

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:15.480
and privilege to work with him.

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:17.730
And I would like to give Matthew the work

00:22:17.730 --> 00:22:19.180
to explain a little bit more.

00:22:21.570 --> 00:22:25.633
- Thank you, Diego, and
good afternoon, everybody.

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:31.000
So as Diego was just explaining,

00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:36.860
the basic rules of
collective animal behavior,

00:22:36.860 --> 00:22:41.000
and this is a composer
explaining to scientists,

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:44.590
which I acknowledge, is
that animals want to move

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:49.520
in a common average direction,
common average speed,

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:51.750
and common average
distance from each other.

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:53.300
Those are the rules of harmony.

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:58.660
That's how I teach students how to compose

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:01.810
a chorale in the style of Bach.

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:04.030
They're the rules that Palestrina used,

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:05.560
and they're still the same rules

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:07.810
that we use to write pop songs.

00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:12.060
So if I talk a little bit

00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:17.020
about the ideas behind
it, so when I look at,

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:20.490
say, the murmuration of starlings,

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:25.490
you saw that, a video that
Diego showed earlier on,

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:28.717
I look at that, and I think,
"That looks like music,"

00:23:30.110 --> 00:23:33.540
but it's not a trivial thing to work out

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:35.850
how to take that
three-dimensional behavior,

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:39.970
even if you have really
elaborate exact tracking data,

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:43.520
and then map that onto musical parameters.

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:47.870
So by musical parameters,
I mean pitch, rhythm,

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:50.950
duration, articulation, phrasing,

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:54.320
timbre, tone color, et cetera.

00:23:54.320 --> 00:23:56.550
So the first place or the obvious place

00:23:56.550 --> 00:24:01.550
that we wanted to explore is
that if we have, for example,

00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:08.680
XY coordinates on a Cartesian plane

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:11.160
tracking individual animals,

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:14.080
well, then we could map
that to, say, pitch.

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:18.500
And in music theory, we
talk about pitch space,

00:24:18.500 --> 00:24:22.154
and in this case, we're looking
at what's called a Tonnetz,

00:24:22.154 --> 00:24:26.770
which is 19th century music
theory, this is not new.

00:24:26.770 --> 00:24:30.310
And you can see that moving left to right,

00:24:30.310 --> 00:24:35.310
we've got, on the horizontal,
we have perfect fifths.

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:36.770
And then on the diagonal,

00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:41.350
we have various kinds of
thirds, major and minor thirds.

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:43.880
And if you look at the
triangulation in the middle,

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:47.370
C, E flat, G, that's a C minor triad.

00:24:47.370 --> 00:24:49.500
And then if you flip underneath,

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:52.050
C, G, E, that's a C major triad.

00:24:52.050 --> 00:24:55.450
So there's a way in which you
can theoretically navigate

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:59.923
around a pitch space and
map coordinates to it.

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:03.970
We're even, started to look
at more complex shapes.

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:08.970
Here we've got a Tonnetz
in the shape of a torus,

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:12.390
which are mathematical friends,

00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:14.190
and sisters actually,
still two-dimensional.

00:25:14.190 --> 00:25:15.450
I wanted to call it three-dimensional

00:25:15.450 --> 00:25:20.143
and was (laughs) quickly disused of that.

00:25:21.677 --> 00:25:22.510
And so as I mentioned,

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:26.800
this is not a trivial thing to work out.

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:29.610
Well, how do you make
music from this data?

00:25:29.610 --> 00:25:31.890
So we've had some breakthroughs recently

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:33.910
that I'd like to share,

00:25:33.910 --> 00:25:38.910
just to give you a taste
of where we are heading.

00:25:40.010 --> 00:25:45.010
So here I have the beginnings of,

00:25:48.770 --> 00:25:50.520
taking the data that we have

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:54.710
for a group of 10 homing pigeons.

00:25:54.710 --> 00:25:58.590
And so the data

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:02.010
is around 150,000 lines,

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:03.720
which includes XY coordinates

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:06.020
and also other information to work out

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:09.610
whether they're milling,
swarming, or they're polarized.

00:26:09.610 --> 00:26:13.053
And so simply what I've
done is we've taken that,

00:26:14.510 --> 00:26:18.330
the Cartesian coordinates,
and the breakthrough,

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:21.580
which actually came, well,
how do we visualize this?

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:25.360
And through thinking through
visualizing it for you,

00:26:25.360 --> 00:26:28.580
we mapped the Cartesian
coordinates to polar coordinates.

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:30.330
And then lo and behold, we found a way

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:33.560
of mapping that to pitch space.

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:35.880
So what I'm gonna play
here, which is actually run,

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:38.700
is gonna run in real time,
it's not a recording,

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:41.840
is taking the data of XY coordinates,

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:43.340
turning them to polar coordinates,

00:26:43.340 --> 00:26:45.010
and then mapping that to a pitch space,

00:26:45.010 --> 00:26:47.300
and then sending that to another program,

00:26:47.300 --> 00:26:50.080
which is gonna play back chorale samples.

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:54.940
So now we're in theory
have a choir of 10 pigeons,

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:58.920
and it, to me, it sounds like music,

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:02.560
but that may be debatable,
but it's a first step.

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:03.784
Here we go.

00:27:03.784 --> 00:27:07.201
(inspiring choral music)

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:37.870
So that's the beginning of something that,

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:40.840
at least from a
compositional point of view,

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:45.200
I really do feel that we've
got something to work with.

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:49.070
So just to show you
briefly what it looks like

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:52.390
in terms of actual music,

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:57.390
so what we were playing then
was recorded in real time,

00:27:59.490 --> 00:28:04.470
and there we have pigeons,

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:07.570
homing pigeons producing chords

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:09.890
that if I were to look at this,

00:28:09.890 --> 00:28:12.940
I would say, "This is music."

00:28:12.940 --> 00:28:16.633
And so I'm pretty excited
about the potential for this.

00:28:18.550 --> 00:28:22.934
The next step, of course,
would be to take this

00:28:22.934 --> 00:28:27.800
and then map the movement
onto a surround sound system,

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:30.860
so a kind of cinematic experience.

00:28:30.860 --> 00:28:33.740
It might even extend as far as, say,

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:36.880
a virtual reality experience
where you could be a pigeon

00:28:37.861 --> 00:28:40.750
in the flock of pigeons while listening

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:45.650
to the realization of this.

00:28:45.650 --> 00:28:48.810
So this, we're really excited for this,

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:53.810
and something that was an
unexpected thing for me

00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:59.500
is to look at, say, these
recordings of environments

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:01.680
that Diego's gonna be getting into

00:29:03.458 --> 00:29:06.130
and making musical realizations of those.

00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:08.737
So it's not just the flocking
or the schooling data,

00:29:08.737 --> 00:29:12.770
but actually taking
environmental recordings

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:15.400
and then mapping that onto musical spaces,

00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:19.890
so the sonification and
visualization of data,

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:22.740
which is something that
I'm really interested in

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:26.180
as a way of showing people

00:29:26.180 --> 00:29:28.670
what is happening in the environment.

00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:30.320
So it's one thing to sort of say,

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:33.300
you know, "Here's what's going on"

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:36.750
in terms of some kind
of abstract description.

00:29:36.750 --> 00:29:39.417
It's another thing to
express it through art.

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:42.290
So that's part of the project,

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:44.730
and I'm really excited
to be working with Diego,

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:47.477
and I will hand it back to him, thank you.

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:54.623
- Thank thank you, Matthew.

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:00.680
We are not the first people

00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:03.090
to talk about data sonifications.

00:30:03.090 --> 00:30:06.270
NASA has been working on
this for quite some time.

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:08.374
Here we can see what
the galaxy sounds like.

00:30:08.374 --> 00:30:09.930
(calm music)

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:11.880
Different colors are different pitches.

00:30:14.637 --> 00:30:19.637
(calm music)
(chimes tinkling)

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:28.300
But I do believe that one
of the major advantages

00:30:28.300 --> 00:30:33.300
and progresses of this project
is that we are applying

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:36.480
both musical theory and biological theory,

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:39.573
for example, as the Tonnetz
that Matthew has mentioned.

00:30:40.460 --> 00:30:42.840
We are also adding, we've performed-

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:45.480
(jarring music)

00:30:45.480 --> 00:30:48.650
Apologies, we're also
exploring different ways

00:30:48.650 --> 00:30:51.460
of playing with chord
progressions and feelings.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:54.870
For example, as some of
these collective states

00:30:54.870 --> 00:30:57.350
in which animals can be such as swarming

00:30:57.350 --> 00:31:00.680
or whether they are transitioning
from one state to another,

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:03.930
these are somewhat more
chaotic, or we can, for example,

00:31:03.930 --> 00:31:07.330
add different chord progressions
to them to evoke emotions

00:31:07.330 --> 00:31:11.310
as animals change from
a more chaotic system,

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:13.663
such as swarming, to a polarized state.

00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:17.500
And in addition, we are
exploring new ways of,

00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:19.910
such as the, we call it the pigeon reader,

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:21.010
what Matthew just showed you,

00:31:21.010 --> 00:31:24.080
almost of creating new
synthetic synthesizer

00:31:24.080 --> 00:31:26.960
or instruments that artists could use.

00:31:26.960 --> 00:31:28.380
And the interesting part of this

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:31.310
is that this would represent

00:31:31.310 --> 00:31:34.420
a direct connection of
nature with an artist.

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:36.760
So it actually really physically matters

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:38.120
where the artist is in space

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:41.260
and how the output of some of
this music might sound like.

00:31:41.260 --> 00:31:44.060
And I'm going to jump a little bit

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:46.000
into some variables of interest

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:48.160
that we've been interested as biologists,

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:51.290
and I will showcase you four
different examples of music

00:31:51.290 --> 00:31:53.160
that was generated by different animals

00:31:53.160 --> 00:31:55.220
and how this has a little bit evolved

00:31:55.220 --> 00:31:56.800
in complexity through time

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:58.950
as we've learned so
much from this project.

00:31:59.960 --> 00:32:02.030
If we have a group of fish, for example,

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:03.560
we could look at a single fish.

00:32:03.560 --> 00:32:05.840
We could look at how fast are you going,

00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:08.290
how fast is this fish accelerating,

00:32:08.290 --> 00:32:10.710
what's the distance between its next fish.

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:13.070
We could also look at
the collective and say,

00:32:13.070 --> 00:32:14.840
what's the average
speed of the collective,

00:32:14.840 --> 00:32:18.520
where's the collective average position,

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:21.070
what's the degree of alignment

00:32:21.070 --> 00:32:23.300
among this fish called polarization,

00:32:23.300 --> 00:32:26.660
how much are they moving around
their own axis, rotation,

00:32:26.660 --> 00:32:29.010
is this a collective state that is stable,

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:32.460
such as polarized, or are
they trying to transition.

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:35.190
And I'm going to try to showcase
some of these parameters

00:32:35.190 --> 00:32:38.280
we've been playing around
with with some pigeons.

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:42.330
In this example, we split
our pigeon group into three,

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:44.720
and these three groups of pigeons

00:32:44.720 --> 00:32:46.970
represented three different
notes on a keyboard,

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:49.207
and it's going to sound very clunky.

00:32:49.207 --> 00:32:51.874
(chaotic music)

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:00.470
- [Priya] Diego, we are
losing the sound somehow.

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:01.563
Could you turn it up?

00:33:05.236 --> 00:33:06.613
(discordant music)
- Is this better?

00:33:08.767 --> 00:33:09.934
- [Priya] Yes.

00:33:12.580 --> 00:33:14.700
- So this is a very chaotic, clunky sound

00:33:14.700 --> 00:33:17.390
as displayed in the
art on the bottom left.

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:19.520
And I'm excited to say
that we currently have

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:22.430
an art exhibit opening that's
gonna display all this art,

00:33:22.430 --> 00:33:25.230
and we will show you a little
bit later a glimpse of it.

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:30.260
Our next artists on the ensemble
today are stickleback fish,

00:33:30.260 --> 00:33:34.200
and this is an image of
these fish in a fish tank.

00:33:34.200 --> 00:33:36.590
And I showed this to
an artist at Tsai CITY,

00:33:36.590 --> 00:33:38.730
and he was surprised by
how beautiful this was.

00:33:38.730 --> 00:33:41.450
And I said, "It was made
by the animals themselves.

00:33:41.450 --> 00:33:42.980
This is their reality.

00:33:42.980 --> 00:33:46.720
This was their experience in
couple hours in a fish tank."

00:33:46.720 --> 00:33:50.040
So we are communicating
with different artists

00:33:50.040 --> 00:33:52.070
and sculptors of exploring new ways

00:33:52.070 --> 00:33:54.160
of representing the natural world

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:56.380
through the animal lives themselves.

00:33:56.380 --> 00:33:58.960
And this is, we have
applied the same principle.

00:33:58.960 --> 00:34:01.800
We have a group of I think it
was 10 fish in a fish tank.

00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:03.950
We have split this into groups of three.

00:34:03.950 --> 00:34:05.530
So if you're closer to others,

00:34:05.530 --> 00:34:08.720
you will become one key on a pitch space,

00:34:08.720 --> 00:34:11.740
as Matthew was explaining,
and as the fish moves,

00:34:11.740 --> 00:34:13.290
the different keys will change.

00:34:16.436 --> 00:34:19.353
(discordant music)

00:34:22.444 --> 00:34:25.027
(somber music)

00:34:38.243 --> 00:34:41.950
And it's very interesting
because you can even listen

00:34:41.950 --> 00:34:44.240
that these animals are
in a different medium.

00:34:44.240 --> 00:34:46.690
Fish can turn around
because they're in water.

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:50.140
A pigeon can't just make a
full stop and then turn around.

00:34:50.140 --> 00:34:52.640
So we can even listen to different mediums

00:34:52.640 --> 00:34:56.280
in which these animals live
and experience the world.

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:58.070
These are the actual homing pigeon

00:34:58.070 --> 00:34:59.210
that Matthew was showing you.

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:03.740
These were tracked with GPS
collars, and every single one,

00:35:03.740 --> 00:35:05.610
and they're part actually
of a seminal paper

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:07.980
that discovered leadership in pigeons,

00:35:07.980 --> 00:35:09.800
who's leading a flock of pigeons.

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:12.720
So we're actually using
cutting-edge scientific data

00:35:12.720 --> 00:35:14.623
for this data certification.

00:35:16.270 --> 00:35:20.730
This is a previous version
of Matthew's pigeon reader,

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:22.960
and on the top right, we can see the art.

00:35:22.960 --> 00:35:25.640
This is the actual tracks
and the actual data,

00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:29.040
so the raw Excel sheet
plotted on a landscape.

00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:32.580
It looks like a artist just made a splash,

00:35:32.580 --> 00:35:35.170
and every single color is
a different individual.

00:35:35.170 --> 00:35:37.920
And here we're going to
see Matthew performing

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:40.490
and practicing live at
our opening at Tsai CITY

00:35:40.490 --> 00:35:41.390
earlier this week.

00:35:48.290 --> 00:35:51.707
(inspiring choral music)

00:35:54.260 --> 00:35:55.313
It was so much fun.

00:35:57.980 --> 00:36:00.270
And here we have my personal favorites.

00:36:00.270 --> 00:36:02.680
These are eight termites on a Petri dish.

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:05.430
And here I wanna highlight and showcase

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:09.530
how our artificial intelligence
computer algorithm works,

00:36:09.530 --> 00:36:13.720
namely, it's able to track
every single termite,

00:36:13.720 --> 00:36:15.600
and each different termite is tracked

00:36:15.600 --> 00:36:19.450
in its position and its time
and it's unique identity.

00:36:19.450 --> 00:36:23.570
And what we get out of
this is the raw data

00:36:23.570 --> 00:36:27.220
of a time, a frame, a position in x space,

00:36:27.220 --> 00:36:29.840
a position of y space, and the identity.

00:36:29.840 --> 00:36:31.210
And this is the raw building block

00:36:31.210 --> 00:36:33.480
for a lot of these musical parameters

00:36:33.480 --> 00:36:36.110
and collective behavior
parameters that we're creating.

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:37.890
This means that we can make this

00:36:37.890 --> 00:36:40.040
for almost any single animal now on.

00:36:40.040 --> 00:36:42.750
and we have the code, and we
are planning to make the code

00:36:42.750 --> 00:36:44.450
fully available in public in GitHub.

00:36:44.450 --> 00:36:47.010
We want everyone to be able to
use this and claim by no way

00:36:47.010 --> 00:36:49.390
that we should be the
only ones doing this.

00:36:49.390 --> 00:36:54.230
And in this piece made by
eight different termites,

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:57.320
we have an individual termite

00:36:57.320 --> 00:37:00.110
on I think it was on a guitar, yes.

00:37:00.110 --> 00:37:02.370
And then we have three
different parameters,

00:37:02.370 --> 00:37:04.940
collective behavior parameters of termites

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:08.130
translated into music and
three different pianos.

00:37:08.130 --> 00:37:10.410
So the only thing I did as a human being

00:37:10.410 --> 00:37:11.850
was assign different instruments

00:37:11.850 --> 00:37:14.640
to different parameters of these ants.

00:37:16.674 --> 00:37:19.257
(lively music)

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:58.200
And besides just computer
vision, artificial intelligence,

00:37:58.200 --> 00:38:00.400
I also mentioned these
have incredible advantage

00:38:00.400 --> 00:38:02.240
in sound recognition.

00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:05.470
And for this we've put
little birds by microphones

00:38:05.470 --> 00:38:08.530
and computers, Raspberry Pis and AudioMoth

00:38:08.530 --> 00:38:12.620
with $9 microphones from Amazon,
the cheapest I could find,

00:38:12.620 --> 00:38:15.060
and we put them on the
Yale Farm for a month,

00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:16.580
and we're planning on keeping them up

00:38:16.580 --> 00:38:19.100
a little bit longer to
document bird migration.

00:38:19.100 --> 00:38:22.520
And what was incredible is
that we detected 60 species

00:38:22.520 --> 00:38:25.100
at the farm in this four-week experiment

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:28.670
and documented over 20,000 observations.

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:30.900
And if you're wondering
who was the loudest

00:38:30.900 --> 00:38:33.740
and most common bird, you guessed right.

00:38:33.740 --> 00:38:37.590
It was the blue jay, very loud
bird, very beautiful bird.

00:38:37.590 --> 00:38:40.010
And what this AI also allows us to do

00:38:40.010 --> 00:38:43.410
is it allows us to document the species

00:38:43.410 --> 00:38:46.600
but also a confidence
of how certain the AI is

00:38:46.600 --> 00:38:49.100
that it has documented a certain species.

00:38:49.100 --> 00:38:51.300
So beyond musical interest,

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:54.140
this is incredibly exciting
for bird biodiversity

00:38:54.140 --> 00:38:57.980
because we can document
biodiversity through time

00:38:57.980 --> 00:39:01.150
24 hours a day for under $200.

00:39:01.150 --> 00:39:04.130
So this could be and is
currently a game changer

00:39:04.130 --> 00:39:05.680
in biodiversity research.

00:39:05.680 --> 00:39:09.120
Especially thinking and
communicating about this work

00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:14.120
with artists, musicians,
activists, it has changed the way

00:39:14.170 --> 00:39:16.450
that I see a little bit my own science,

00:39:16.450 --> 00:39:20.110
because if we can use all these tools

00:39:20.110 --> 00:39:22.270
to educate the next
generation of scientists,

00:39:22.270 --> 00:39:24.880
policymakers, and musicians, children,

00:39:24.880 --> 00:39:27.760
we could empower them
to collect and sample

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:29.500
and listen to their neighborhoods,

00:39:29.500 --> 00:39:31.460
learn computer programming on the way,

00:39:31.460 --> 00:39:33.350
which is a really important skill,

00:39:33.350 --> 00:39:34.700
learn about the natural world,

00:39:34.700 --> 00:39:36.850
become stewards of their environment,

00:39:36.850 --> 00:39:39.620
have a lot of fun doing
it, spend time outside,

00:39:39.620 --> 00:39:41.870
which has been really
important during COVID,

00:39:41.870 --> 00:39:43.870
and make music and art out of it.

00:39:43.870 --> 00:39:45.610
So this is called SHTEAM.

00:39:45.610 --> 00:39:47.803
So it's STEM, humanities and art.

00:39:48.780 --> 00:39:52.940
And I'm thrilled that Tsai
CITY gave us the opportunity

00:39:52.940 --> 00:39:55.230
to open an art display this week

00:39:55.230 --> 00:39:56.410
in this incredible building.

00:39:56.410 --> 00:39:58.260
And it's open until Friday morning,

00:39:58.260 --> 00:40:00.220
so please check it out
if you have the time.

00:40:00.220 --> 00:40:01.870
The building actually
looks like a fish tank

00:40:01.870 --> 00:40:02.763
and a bird nest.

00:40:04.082 --> 00:40:06.032
And we opened this award, and this was,

00:40:07.373 --> 00:40:09.870
as a basic scientist,
this was my imagination

00:40:09.870 --> 00:40:12.130
of what this art exhibit
could've looked like,

00:40:12.130 --> 00:40:13.080
I'd never done this,

00:40:13.080 --> 00:40:15.030
and I think we actually came quite close.

00:40:15.030 --> 00:40:18.450
So I invite you to take a
collective plunge with me here.

00:40:18.450 --> 00:40:20.140
This is the building.

00:40:20.140 --> 00:40:23.910
Here we have someone exploring
the birds by microphone,

00:40:23.910 --> 00:40:27.050
trying to play a bird sound and
see if it can be documented.

00:40:27.050 --> 00:40:28.750
To the right side, we have a little poster

00:40:28.750 --> 00:40:30.650
describing what we found at the farm,

00:40:30.650 --> 00:40:32.170
who the loudest singer was,

00:40:32.170 --> 00:40:35.580
who ate the farm compost,
scientific communication.

00:40:35.580 --> 00:40:36.900
In the back, we have the music

00:40:36.900 --> 00:40:39.433
generated by our eight termite artists.

00:40:40.870 --> 00:40:43.270
In addition, as I mentioned,
the next generation

00:40:44.173 --> 00:40:45.410
is what matters the most

00:40:45.410 --> 00:40:48.350
because they will follow
up with this work.

00:40:48.350 --> 00:40:50.890
And I'm very proud to say that we launched

00:40:50.890 --> 00:40:54.750
an art exhibit contest
throughout New Haven

00:40:54.750 --> 00:40:57.930
asking children of any
age to paint any wildlife

00:40:57.930 --> 00:40:59.790
in Connecticut, and we made flyers

00:40:59.790 --> 00:41:01.190
both in English and Spanish,

00:41:01.190 --> 00:41:03.400
and we were overwhelmed by the talent,

00:41:03.400 --> 00:41:06.100
the incredible imagination of the artists.

00:41:06.100 --> 00:41:07.520
And here are the winners.

00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:09.610
We have Lillian Delgado who documented

00:41:09.610 --> 00:41:11.910
every single owl in Connecticut.

00:41:11.910 --> 00:41:14.400
We have Leslie Pawlak, a second grader,

00:41:14.400 --> 00:41:16.530
documenting birds and mammals.

00:41:16.530 --> 00:41:19.250
And we have Alina Zhang
making a 3D chicken,

00:41:19.250 --> 00:41:21.520
and she sent me several
pictures of the process,

00:41:21.520 --> 00:41:24.010
which was just mind-blowing.

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:25.960
And here we have all the other artists.

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:30.170
And I have to say that because
we made this advertisement

00:41:30.170 --> 00:41:33.620
in Spanish, we got 40% of emails

00:41:33.620 --> 00:41:35.920
and submissions that were in Spanish.

00:41:35.920 --> 00:41:39.770
And the robin was the
most common animal drawn.

00:41:39.770 --> 00:41:41.420
And as a biodiversity scientist,

00:41:41.420 --> 00:41:43.180
it's interesting that birds and mammals

00:41:43.180 --> 00:41:46.823
were the most frequent ones
being painted by children.

00:41:48.270 --> 00:41:53.270
And I'm trying to think
about a more 10,000-foot view

00:41:53.730 --> 00:41:55.662
about this whole concept.

00:41:55.662 --> 00:41:58.880
I love the idea of generating
new ways of visualizing

00:41:58.880 --> 00:42:01.970
the natural world and sonifying it

00:42:01.970 --> 00:42:05.090
and bringing it back into the
hands of the human artists

00:42:05.090 --> 00:42:08.466
And this is, for example,
how our little microphones

00:42:08.466 --> 00:42:10.330
(screechy music)

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:12.910
in real time can be put into the hands

00:42:12.910 --> 00:42:14.650
of artists such as Matthew.

00:42:14.650 --> 00:42:18.240
And I have to say that last
week was the first time ever

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:21.670
that we practiced music because
of the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:42:21.670 --> 00:42:23.600
Most of this work has been done on Zoom,

00:42:23.600 --> 00:42:27.420
and it was an absolute
pleasure to just brainstorm

00:42:27.420 --> 00:42:29.253
and code and make music together.

00:42:30.410 --> 00:42:34.860
We're incredibly lucky to
have been supported by artists

00:42:34.860 --> 00:42:38.060
and cancer survivors of
Yale Smilow Hospital.

00:42:38.060 --> 00:42:42.950
These are origami cranes made
by patients in the hospital,

00:42:42.950 --> 00:42:45.110
and there's a Japanese tradition

00:42:45.110 --> 00:42:47.120
that if there's a thousand origami cranes,

00:42:47.120 --> 00:42:48.610
all your dreams will become true,

00:42:48.610 --> 00:42:50.210
and you will have good health.

00:42:50.210 --> 00:42:52.620
And I'm very grateful
that these incredible

00:42:52.620 --> 00:42:53.820
art pieces were lent to us.

00:42:53.820 --> 00:42:55.980
You see them at the Smilow Hospital.

00:42:55.980 --> 00:42:58.690
And I love the fact that
they're in pride colors.

00:42:58.690 --> 00:43:03.300
And we also got incredibly
Smilow rocks that just,

00:43:05.000 --> 00:43:06.460
these are the moments that remind me

00:43:06.460 --> 00:43:09.040
that before a scientist,
I'm also human being,

00:43:09.040 --> 00:43:12.480
and just reading these
just springs my curiosity

00:43:12.480 --> 00:43:14.960
into a million pieces, and
I'm going to read some of them

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:17.127
because they're so powerful.

00:43:17.127 --> 00:43:19.240
"Dance to your own music.

00:43:19.240 --> 00:43:21.053
The birds sing even when it rains.

00:43:21.963 --> 00:43:24.803
The song of the earth
writes the music of my soul.

00:43:25.750 --> 00:43:30.120
Listen closely, even the trees
exhale their own love songs.

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:32.590
Sing the song you were born to sing.

00:43:32.590 --> 00:43:35.410
Be still and listen.
The earth is singing."

00:43:35.410 --> 00:43:40.410
And these moments are,
besides my being a scientist,

00:43:41.140 --> 00:43:42.540
these moments just bring me back

00:43:42.540 --> 00:43:46.470
and make me think so much bigger
than my scientific research

00:43:46.470 --> 00:43:48.770
and what the impact
could be of this project,

00:43:48.770 --> 00:43:50.956
also to bring joy to people

00:43:50.956 --> 00:43:54.193
and promote education
and equity in a just way.

00:43:55.140 --> 00:43:58.750
We also were very lucky
to have three art pieces

00:43:58.750 --> 00:44:01.970
by local artists and scientists

00:44:01.970 --> 00:44:04.000
that are on display in the art gallery.

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:07.070
And these are just some of the
images, incredible building.

00:44:07.070 --> 00:44:08.990
If you have the chance, please go.

00:44:08.990 --> 00:44:11.560
And this is Matthew and me performing live

00:44:11.560 --> 00:44:15.143
for the first time ever with
the pigeon Gregorian choir.

00:44:16.130 --> 00:44:18.510
Here's a colleague, as a collective pulse,

00:44:18.510 --> 00:44:20.360
trying to change the stream of music,

00:44:20.360 --> 00:44:21.653
and that's totally fine.

00:44:25.090 --> 00:44:29.650
And this is another fact of so
many things that I've learned

00:44:29.650 --> 00:44:31.723
by working with humanities artists,

00:44:32.890 --> 00:44:35.160
I invited some of my
friends who were dancers

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:38.740
to participate, and I said,
"If you come, you can dance."

00:44:38.740 --> 00:44:41.120
So they did, and so I did.

00:44:41.120 --> 00:44:44.690
And this just sprung my
creativity to gave me new ideas

00:44:44.690 --> 00:44:45.900
and make me a better scientist.

00:44:45.900 --> 00:44:48.603
What if we map all these
things into dancers?

00:44:51.540 --> 00:44:53.030
The last piece of music I will play today

00:44:53.030 --> 00:44:56.230
is a sneak peek into a
electronic music production

00:44:56.230 --> 00:44:58.170
made by the termites where, again,

00:44:58.170 --> 00:45:01.060
the only parameters that
I decided as a human

00:45:01.060 --> 00:45:02.940
were the instruments they play.

00:45:02.940 --> 00:45:05.740
And here we have four
different colors of ants,

00:45:05.740 --> 00:45:08.270
which just means that I've
looked into four different

00:45:08.270 --> 00:45:11.080
patterns of the entire group at once

00:45:11.080 --> 00:45:13.230
and mapped them into
different music space.

00:45:14.598 --> 00:45:17.348
(dramatic music)

00:45:19.964 --> 00:45:22.547
(lively music)

00:45:54.856 --> 00:45:57.773
(harmonious music)

00:46:21.989 --> 00:46:24.450
And to bring this back together,

00:46:24.450 --> 00:46:27.120
what I've learned
through this art exhibit,

00:46:27.120 --> 00:46:29.940
by collaborating with scientists, artists,

00:46:29.940 --> 00:46:32.650
musicians, having an
incredible team behind us,

00:46:32.650 --> 00:46:35.603
everything I've shown today
is a collective effort,

00:46:36.640 --> 00:46:38.840
and the most important people are the ones

00:46:38.840 --> 00:46:40.790
that I will show in my last slide,

00:46:40.790 --> 00:46:43.310
but every single city can probably do

00:46:43.310 --> 00:46:44.910
some of what we've shown today.

00:46:44.910 --> 00:46:49.910
Every city has a school, and
we could teach SHTEAM there.

00:46:50.200 --> 00:46:52.780
Every city has a little
farm or a green space

00:46:52.780 --> 00:46:54.900
or a vacant lot or an urban garden

00:46:54.900 --> 00:46:58.550
where we could document
biodiversity through food justice

00:46:58.550 --> 00:47:00.460
and make music out of a farm.

00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:02.670
I'm excited to make Yale Farm music

00:47:02.670 --> 00:47:05.710
with chicken being hi-hat,
shovels being snares,

00:47:05.710 --> 00:47:08.020
and birds being the melody.

00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:11.140
A lot of places have
hospitals or community centers

00:47:11.140 --> 00:47:15.060
or universities, so I see
this whole presentation

00:47:15.060 --> 00:47:17.500
of "Collective Pulse" that
we've demonstrated today

00:47:17.500 --> 00:47:19.480
as a very larger envelope and umbrella

00:47:19.480 --> 00:47:22.190
of bringing the natural world, technology,

00:47:22.190 --> 00:47:24.150
art, and humanity closer together,

00:47:24.150 --> 00:47:27.200
just like the general
purpose of the Franke Program

00:47:27.200 --> 00:47:29.053
as envisioned by Mr. and Mrs. Franke.

00:47:30.670 --> 00:47:32.623
And you might be asking what's next.

00:47:34.230 --> 00:47:36.520
I've learned a lot about working

00:47:36.520 --> 00:47:39.440
with the humanities in this project.

00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:42.910
It's sprung my creativity, my curiosity.

00:47:42.910 --> 00:47:45.750
I love the fact that a lot
of times artists sit back

00:47:45.750 --> 00:47:48.140
and let people be and explore,

00:47:48.140 --> 00:47:49.410
which is little bit different

00:47:49.410 --> 00:47:51.621
than how we work in the sciences.

00:47:51.621 --> 00:47:53.950
I learned about communication,
being very clear,

00:47:53.950 --> 00:47:57.250
interdisciplinary work,
non-verbal communication,

00:47:57.250 --> 00:47:59.800
data visualization, emotion,

00:47:59.800 --> 00:48:01.803
the subjectivity of interpretation,

00:48:02.790 --> 00:48:05.490
the hard questions artists ask,

00:48:05.490 --> 00:48:08.283
and that small grants make us dream big.

00:48:10.930 --> 00:48:12.750
I've also learned from my dancing friends

00:48:12.750 --> 00:48:14.440
that this is something I wanna do later.

00:48:14.440 --> 00:48:18.170
Maybe each one of these
dancers is a collective,

00:48:18.170 --> 00:48:21.630
and what if we try to reconstruct
through their movement

00:48:21.630 --> 00:48:23.220
the music that they were singing to?

00:48:23.220 --> 00:48:24.440
Is that possible?

00:48:24.440 --> 00:48:25.920
There's gonna be so much variation,

00:48:25.920 --> 00:48:28.430
and each time they
dance will be so unique.

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:29.640
Maybe we can recreate this.

00:48:29.640 --> 00:48:32.173
I have no idea, but I'm
excited to find out.

00:48:33.610 --> 00:48:36.180
I'm excited to explore more the sounds

00:48:36.180 --> 00:48:39.570
of entire ecosystems and how
they have changed through time.

00:48:39.570 --> 00:48:44.100
How do the ecosystems from
1960 sounded of Mr. Krause

00:48:44.100 --> 00:48:46.520
versus how they sound now in 2022?

00:48:46.520 --> 00:48:49.930
And artificial intelligence
allows us to split sounds

00:48:49.930 --> 00:48:52.030
into the natural sounds and human sounds,

00:48:52.030 --> 00:48:53.640
for example, with great ease.

00:48:53.640 --> 00:48:55.070
So I'm very excited about this.

00:48:55.070 --> 00:48:56.930
We could make music
about every national park

00:48:56.930 --> 00:48:57.980
in the United States.

00:48:59.360 --> 00:49:01.920
What does the underwater sound?

00:49:01.920 --> 00:49:04.060
Also something we'll be tackling.

00:49:04.060 --> 00:49:06.210
Exploring more musical chord progression

00:49:06.210 --> 00:49:08.363
with Matthew's mentorship.

00:49:10.260 --> 00:49:12.400
Working more with the local
community in New Haven,

00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:16.230
which is, the biggest excitement

00:49:16.230 --> 00:49:18.350
of this entire work was opening my email

00:49:18.350 --> 00:49:20.100
and seeing artwork from
kids, to be honest.

00:49:20.100 --> 00:49:21.870
That has been the highlight

00:49:21.870 --> 00:49:24.020
of this project to me personally.

00:49:24.020 --> 00:49:26.060
And stay tuned because more is coming.

00:49:26.060 --> 00:49:27.500
We're just getting started.

00:49:27.500 --> 00:49:29.880
We have another opening on Saturday.

00:49:29.880 --> 00:49:31.330
We have a Climate Bauhaus

00:49:31.330 --> 00:49:34.057
at the Center for Collaborative
Arts and Media, please come.

00:49:34.057 --> 00:49:37.760
And this is actually
art made by Dana Karwas,

00:49:37.760 --> 00:49:39.950
but this is made out of termite data.

00:49:39.950 --> 00:49:42.760
It's gonna be a large print,
so please check it out.

00:49:42.760 --> 00:49:45.060
And this is the large team
that I was talking about

00:49:45.060 --> 00:49:46.670
that has made everything happen.

00:49:46.670 --> 00:49:48.040
And please take a second to look

00:49:48.040 --> 00:49:51.050
at the interdisciplinary
nature of these people.

00:49:51.050 --> 00:49:53.910
We've got anything from
people in the hospital,

00:49:53.910 --> 00:49:58.010
local community workers,
engineers, ecologists,

00:49:58.010 --> 00:50:00.670
musicians, artists.

00:50:00.670 --> 00:50:03.480
It's an absolute privilege
to work with all of these.

00:50:03.480 --> 00:50:05.040
And with this, I just wanna say,

00:50:05.040 --> 00:50:06.440
thank you all for listening.

00:50:09.070 --> 00:50:11.470
- Thank you so much, Diego,

00:50:11.470 --> 00:50:15.010
for that absolute;y
fantastic mind-blowing talk.

00:50:15.010 --> 00:50:20.010
And thank you, Matthew, for
helping with the formulation

00:50:20.720 --> 00:50:23.693
and the sonification of
these natural sounds.

00:50:24.730 --> 00:50:29.730
We are, I'm just so proud
that the Franke Program

00:50:29.970 --> 00:50:33.460
helped you get a start on
this absolutely wonderful

00:50:33.460 --> 00:50:36.850
really ambitious project,
and we wish you the best.

00:50:36.850 --> 00:50:39.600
And so here's, you know, let's all clap

00:50:39.600 --> 00:50:41.630
and give you a round of applause.

00:50:41.630 --> 00:50:44.343
We so wish we could've done
this in person as well.

00:50:45.230 --> 00:50:49.540
But Ty, did you wanna take
over and handle the questions?

00:50:49.540 --> 00:50:51.830
I think we have a couple coming.

00:50:51.830 --> 00:50:54.470
- Absolutely, so just a
reminder to the audience

00:50:54.470 --> 00:50:55.730
to please submit your questions,

00:50:55.730 --> 00:50:58.690
not in the chat feature, but in the Q&amp;A,

00:50:58.690 --> 00:51:00.273
and we have one already.

00:51:01.150 --> 00:51:05.140
So Diego, "Thank you for a
fascinating presentation.

00:51:05.140 --> 00:51:07.080
I'm compelled by your statement that music

00:51:07.080 --> 00:51:10.310
can be as powerful a
narrative tool as maps

00:51:10.310 --> 00:51:12.850
for telling the story of ecological change

00:51:12.850 --> 00:51:14.840
over space and time.

00:51:14.840 --> 00:51:17.760
Could you speak more to
the storytelling potential

00:51:17.760 --> 00:51:22.760
of sonifying rather than only
visualizing ecological data?"

00:51:24.140 --> 00:51:26.070
- Sure, I'm gonna answer quickly,

00:51:26.070 --> 00:51:29.030
and probably Matthew has a lot of wisdom

00:51:29.030 --> 00:51:29.990
to share in there, too.

00:51:29.990 --> 00:51:31.290
- Yes, please do, Matthew.

00:51:32.200 --> 00:51:34.890
- The interesting part between, say,

00:51:34.890 --> 00:51:37.920
a video of an animation of
a map, or as we have seen,

00:51:37.920 --> 00:51:41.870
imagine the spraying, slowing
the curve of COVID in 2D

00:51:41.870 --> 00:51:46.310
is that with data certification,
we can generate emotions.

00:51:46.310 --> 00:51:49.360
We can say, or we can almost
anticipate what's next.

00:51:49.360 --> 00:51:51.330
If we find certain patterns in our ears,

00:51:51.330 --> 00:51:53.710
we can say, "Okay, we're going up.

00:51:53.710 --> 00:51:57.184
That must mean maybe that the
bird is going up a thermal

00:51:57.184 --> 00:52:01.030
or I have a heads-up that
is going to go down."

00:52:01.030 --> 00:52:03.720
So the interpretation of music allows us

00:52:03.720 --> 00:52:06.630
to disentangle my subjective view,

00:52:06.630 --> 00:52:09.473
much more complex patterns
in a very interesting way.

00:52:13.424 --> 00:52:17.280
- Yeah, so the interesting
thing about sound in general

00:52:17.280 --> 00:52:20.950
is that you can hear something once

00:52:20.950 --> 00:52:24.110
and then not hear it again
for quite a number of years,

00:52:24.110 --> 00:52:25.880
decades even, hear it again,

00:52:25.880 --> 00:52:27.730
and you're back in the same place

00:52:27.730 --> 00:52:29.070
and time that you were before.

00:52:29.070 --> 00:52:34.033
So we have a remarkable
memory for tone color.

00:52:35.410 --> 00:52:38.803
So there's that, where our
visual memories are unreliable.

00:52:40.098 --> 00:52:42.230
And I once had a fascinating conversation

00:52:42.230 --> 00:52:45.340
with a detective in New York
is that if someone describes

00:52:45.340 --> 00:52:48.680
a crime entirely in visual terms,

00:52:48.680 --> 00:52:50.220
there's something wrong with their story.

00:52:50.220 --> 00:52:53.393
If they do not mention smell or sound,

00:52:55.225 --> 00:52:56.700
something serious is missing.

00:52:56.700 --> 00:52:59.050
So it's an interesting thing.

00:52:59.050 --> 00:53:03.470
The other thing about
sound that is remarkable

00:53:03.470 --> 00:53:05.500
is that you can layer sound.

00:53:05.500 --> 00:53:08.140
You can have multiple layers together,

00:53:08.140 --> 00:53:09.270
and it will make sense.

00:53:09.270 --> 00:53:12.753
I mean, that's literally
the meaning of harmony.

00:53:13.688 --> 00:53:16.180
So there, I think in terms of sonification

00:53:16.180 --> 00:53:19.550
and sort of representing
multiple layers of data,

00:53:19.550 --> 00:53:24.550
then there's tremendous potential there.

00:53:24.660 --> 00:53:26.420
So the way that our
brains work, for example,

00:53:26.420 --> 00:53:28.950
is if you hear, if you
have multiple people

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:30.500
speaking at the same time,

00:53:30.500 --> 00:53:33.110
we have difficulty parsing language,

00:53:33.110 --> 00:53:34.810
but if you have multiple instrumentalists

00:53:34.810 --> 00:53:37.980
playing at the same
time, well, we love that.

00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:41.063
So there's, you know,
there's tremendous potential.

00:53:42.377 --> 00:53:43.740
And I think bringing the two together

00:53:43.740 --> 00:53:47.603
is how does and
sonification work together.

00:53:50.230 --> 00:53:52.840
And another thing that I didn't mention,

00:53:52.840 --> 00:53:55.870
when I work with the raw data, you know,

00:53:55.870 --> 00:53:58.970
whether it's playing
around with the termites,

00:53:58.970 --> 00:54:01.300
and I've worked with Marcelo Dietrich

00:54:02.553 --> 00:54:05.800
at the School of Medicine here,

00:54:05.800 --> 00:54:09.590
he's a neuroscientist
in comparative medicine,

00:54:09.590 --> 00:54:13.640
looking at, there's a little
distressing crying baby mice,

00:54:13.640 --> 00:54:17.840
and then mapping that
onto musical parameters.

00:54:17.840 --> 00:54:20.890
There's something authentic about the data

00:54:20.890 --> 00:54:23.210
because it's producing sounds and shapes

00:54:23.210 --> 00:54:26.020
that I would not have done
on my own as a composer.

00:54:26.020 --> 00:54:28.470
So I'm hearing things anew,

00:54:28.470 --> 00:54:31.050
and I think people possibly recognize

00:54:31.050 --> 00:54:35.260
that even that sort of
initial experimentation

00:54:35.260 --> 00:54:38.290
with the pigeons,
there's something really,

00:54:38.290 --> 00:54:40.113
something's going on there.

00:54:41.130 --> 00:54:44.800
And I think that's what
we're trying to get at.

00:54:44.800 --> 00:54:47.940
It's like, and can you
actually tell the difference

00:54:47.940 --> 00:54:51.170
between these species, whether,

00:54:51.170 --> 00:54:52.003
and in terms of what we are doing,

00:54:52.003 --> 00:54:53.790
it's just, we're making decisions

00:54:53.790 --> 00:54:56.610
in terms of what we're,
the sounds we're using,

00:54:56.610 --> 00:54:59.010
but we're not choosing
the notes or the rhythms.

00:55:00.283 --> 00:55:04.220
And so I think there's
a really interesting

00:55:04.220 --> 00:55:06.590
experiment to be done there.

00:55:06.590 --> 00:55:08.700
But I think the most important thing

00:55:11.370 --> 00:55:15.440
is our memory of placing
sounds in time and place.

00:55:15.440 --> 00:55:20.440
And if you're looking at
the loss of the sound world,

00:55:21.320 --> 00:55:24.160
and this is the work of Bernie Krause,

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:25.380
and he's been doing these recordings

00:55:25.380 --> 00:55:28.960
for years and years and years,
and when you layer them up

00:55:28.960 --> 00:55:32.410
over decades, it's remarkable.

00:55:32.410 --> 00:55:35.800
I mean, of course it's deeply distressing.

00:55:35.800 --> 00:55:38.500
So I think this is one
way to kind of get at that

00:55:38.500 --> 00:55:41.070
and making it clear to the public.

00:55:41.070 --> 00:55:45.360
So that's a wonderful
question. Thank you, Megan.

00:55:47.468 --> 00:55:50.440
- Thank you, okay, from
our next participant,

00:55:50.440 --> 00:55:52.987
another commendation, "Wonderful work."

00:55:52.987 --> 00:55:55.100
"A question about tracking insects,

00:55:55.100 --> 00:55:58.790
especially regarding the
ants and termite work,

00:55:58.790 --> 00:56:02.260
how would you track insects
in their natural environment

00:56:02.260 --> 00:56:04.060
as opposed to Petri dishes?"

00:56:04.060 --> 00:56:05.680
I was wondering the same thing,

00:56:05.680 --> 00:56:08.240
and the participant, I
was thinking especially

00:56:08.240 --> 00:56:13.230
about bees, different
sounds, different relation?

00:56:13.230 --> 00:56:16.950
- Yeah, there, about the
tracking through computer vision,

00:56:16.950 --> 00:56:18.430
there I have to give a shout-out

00:56:18.430 --> 00:56:20.230
to my alma mater and past employer,

00:56:20.230 --> 00:56:22.400
the Max Planck Institute
for Animal Behavior

00:56:22.400 --> 00:56:24.680
and the newly formed Centre for the Study

00:56:24.680 --> 00:56:28.350
of Advanced Collective work
from Iain Couzin and his group

00:56:28.350 --> 00:56:30.770
has been pioneering computer vision

00:56:30.770 --> 00:56:32.733
and tracking of really animals.

00:56:33.580 --> 00:56:35.543
I would recommend reading papers.

00:56:36.450 --> 00:56:39.173
There's a computer vision
package called Tracktor.

00:56:40.580 --> 00:56:43.460
There's a computer vision
package called TRex

00:56:43.460 --> 00:56:48.460
from Tristan Walter and Iain
Couzin, eLife 2018, I think.

00:56:48.560 --> 00:56:52.110
And then there's also a
paper and a Python package

00:56:52.110 --> 00:56:55.700
called DeepPoseKit from Blair Costelloe,

00:56:55.700 --> 00:56:57.220
Iain Couzin, and a few other people.

00:56:57.220 --> 00:57:00.240
So I would start by getting
a very clear background.

00:57:00.240 --> 00:57:02.600
I'm not an insects person at all.

00:57:02.600 --> 00:57:04.760
I work with ginormous tortoises

00:57:04.760 --> 00:57:06.330
that move very slow and live forever.

00:57:06.330 --> 00:57:09.580
That's why (laughs) I have
it easy in my research.

00:57:09.580 --> 00:57:11.830
But those were places I would explore

00:57:11.830 --> 00:57:14.810
with Centre for the Advanced
Study of Animal Collective.

00:57:14.810 --> 00:57:16.180
They probably have a couple answers.

00:57:16.180 --> 00:57:19.860
They're working with and building bridges

00:57:19.860 --> 00:57:22.390
and how the collective helps shape

00:57:22.390 --> 00:57:24.720
these structures that are bionic in time

00:57:24.720 --> 00:57:27.360
to work with social networks of bees.

00:57:27.360 --> 00:57:29.160
That would be a good starting point.

00:57:30.660 --> 00:57:32.997
- Thank you, next question.

00:57:32.997 --> 00:57:35.110
"Do animals change their behavior

00:57:35.110 --> 00:57:38.340
from the sound they
generate using your code?

00:57:38.340 --> 00:57:40.740
It would be cool to know whether they move

00:57:40.740 --> 00:57:43.240
to make sounds they like or enjoy,

00:57:43.240 --> 00:57:47.070
so working on a feedback there,

00:57:47.070 --> 00:57:49.557
and again, cheers for an
amazing presentation."

00:57:51.030 --> 00:57:52.350
- That's a really good question.

00:57:52.350 --> 00:57:54.970
I'm not 100% sure I understand it.

00:57:54.970 --> 00:57:58.400
But so there's a lot of research

00:57:58.400 --> 00:58:02.180
looking at playback experiments
to see, for example,

00:58:02.180 --> 00:58:06.290
how would zebra react if it hears a lion?

00:58:06.290 --> 00:58:08.590
So that sort of research
has been done extensively,

00:58:08.590 --> 00:58:11.673
also to look at how plastic
animal responses are.

00:58:13.010 --> 00:58:16.450
Whether or not animals like certain music,

00:58:16.450 --> 00:58:17.283
I am not sure.

00:58:17.283 --> 00:58:20.310
I'm sure there's a lot
of psychological research

00:58:20.310 --> 00:58:23.223
into whether dogs like
classical music or not.

00:58:24.250 --> 00:58:26.090
I would have to see. That's
a really good question.

00:58:26.090 --> 00:58:29.700
I might try it out with my
neighbor's dog. (laughs)

00:58:29.700 --> 00:58:33.040
- Okay, "But music
software was used to map

00:58:33.040 --> 00:58:36.510
the termite music, Logic, mainstream,

00:58:36.510 --> 00:58:38.250
GarageBand, et cetera."

00:58:40.230 --> 00:58:44.040
- And we used Ableton,
and in its simplest form,

00:58:44.040 --> 00:58:46.900
the video that you see
there was GarageBand,

00:58:46.900 --> 00:58:50.600
where we literally just dragged
almost randomly instruments

00:58:50.600 --> 00:58:52.310
to see if it sounded
and sounded beautiful.

00:58:52.310 --> 00:58:55.430
But most of the more complex patterns

00:58:55.430 --> 00:58:58.390
that Matthew is using is
from a software Ableton.

00:58:59.593 --> 00:59:01.707
- Okay, and a follow-up question
from the same participant,

00:59:01.707 --> 00:59:04.850
"And for the termites, was
pitch mostly determined

00:59:04.850 --> 00:59:07.200
by position of the termites?

00:59:07.200 --> 00:59:09.870
If so, how were other parts of the music

00:59:09.870 --> 00:59:13.020
like volume determined?"

00:59:13.020 --> 00:59:15.530
Matthew, and you wanna interject here?

00:59:17.670 --> 00:59:21.030
- Sorry, sorry, where's
this? Which question is this?

00:59:21.030 --> 00:59:23.087
- Sorry, Aaron's question here,

00:59:23.087 --> 00:59:26.403
"Was pitch mostly determined
by position of the termites?

00:59:27.271 --> 00:59:29.230
And if so, how were
other parts of the music

00:59:29.230 --> 00:59:31.457
like volume determined?"

00:59:33.430 --> 00:59:35.690
- Yeah, so I think in
the case of the termites,

00:59:35.690 --> 00:59:39.930
it was mostly coordinate position.

00:59:39.930 --> 00:59:44.930
And some of the way in
which the music software

00:59:46.340 --> 00:59:51.340
interacts with that is also
producing nuance, which is,

00:59:51.620 --> 00:59:56.620
you know, accents and dynamic variation.

00:59:57.420 --> 00:59:58.807
So that's not, in that case,

00:59:58.807 --> 01:00:00.780
that's not really coming directly

01:00:07.225 --> 01:00:08.133
from the termites.

01:00:08.133 --> 01:00:10.030
I mean, it could, 'cause
we have enough data

01:00:10.030 --> 01:00:11.840
to map that onto it.

01:00:11.840 --> 01:00:15.600
- And in one of the videos
though, the one where you see

01:00:15.600 --> 01:00:19.080
the Petri dish and the
musical instruments there,

01:00:19.080 --> 01:00:22.490
shout-out to Jon Koss,
who's electric engineer

01:00:22.490 --> 01:00:23.800
helping with this project.

01:00:23.800 --> 01:00:26.930
We actually were able to
map chord progressions

01:00:26.930 --> 01:00:29.110
based on the collective
state of the system.

01:00:29.110 --> 01:00:33.057
So if at a given time point,
say our Excel sheet says,

01:00:33.057 --> 01:00:35.040
"These eight termites
are in polarized state,"

01:00:35.040 --> 01:00:36.560
it would play different chords.

01:00:36.560 --> 01:00:39.140
Then if 20 rows later, all the termites

01:00:39.140 --> 01:00:40.500
are like far away from each other,

01:00:40.500 --> 01:00:42.070
that would be a transition state.

01:00:42.070 --> 01:00:44.970
So we are actually able
to, as Matthew said,

01:00:44.970 --> 01:00:46.560
the pitch is determined by the position,

01:00:46.560 --> 01:00:49.040
but also some of the chords are determined

01:00:49.040 --> 01:00:50.560
by the collective state of the system,

01:00:50.560 --> 01:00:51.570
which is really interesting

01:00:51.570 --> 01:00:54.840
because we're playing with
the group and the individual,

01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:56.980
so you could almost think in future work,

01:00:56.980 --> 01:00:59.870
someone making the background
noise and a single pigeon

01:00:59.870 --> 01:01:01.620
or a individual playing the melody.

01:01:02.550 --> 01:01:04.740
- Yeah, I mean, there's a little bit

01:01:04.740 --> 01:01:08.650
of some kind of accidental discovery.

01:01:08.650 --> 01:01:12.450
So when the animals move
from position to position,

01:01:12.450 --> 01:01:15.670
the velocity of that movement can produce

01:01:15.670 --> 01:01:17.500
really interesting musical results,

01:01:17.500 --> 01:01:19.430
which was surprising to us.

01:01:19.430 --> 01:01:22.840
So that's not programmed in directly.

01:01:22.840 --> 01:01:24.550
That's coming from the interaction

01:01:24.550 --> 01:01:27.130
between the data and the musical software.

01:01:27.130 --> 01:01:31.210
So there's a gift, you
know, happy accidents.

01:01:31.210 --> 01:01:33.763
But we could, the next step, for example,

01:01:34.980 --> 01:01:38.500
is to look at, say, velocity and position

01:01:38.500 --> 01:01:41.410
to not only control pitch

01:01:41.410 --> 01:01:45.720
but to control spectral information.

01:01:45.720 --> 01:01:49.885
So in any musical sound,
there's going to be,

01:01:49.885 --> 01:01:51.560
you know, harmonic content,

01:01:51.560 --> 01:01:54.420
and that harmonic content
will change over time.

01:01:54.420 --> 01:01:58.140
That produces a nuance and shape

01:01:59.037 --> 01:02:02.150
and a single musical note, for example.

01:02:02.150 --> 01:02:04.660
So that's the, I think there's something,

01:02:04.660 --> 01:02:05.780
there's a lot of potential there

01:02:05.780 --> 01:02:09.720
when you're looking at, say,
the murmuration of starlings

01:02:09.720 --> 01:02:11.350
where you get these sort of darker shapes

01:02:11.350 --> 01:02:14.077
and shading and things like that.

01:02:14.077 --> 01:02:15.600
I mean, there's so many animals,

01:02:15.600 --> 01:02:19.730
it wouldn't make sense to map
them all onto a single pitch,

01:02:19.730 --> 01:02:22.980
but you could control
like the filtering of it.

01:02:22.980 --> 01:02:27.340
So we are also getting
into psychoacoustics here.

01:02:27.340 --> 01:02:30.100
So there's a kind of
psychological testing element

01:02:30.100 --> 01:02:34.840
to this, which is potentially
really exciting, too.

01:02:34.840 --> 01:02:36.240
So going back to the original question,

01:02:36.240 --> 01:02:39.860
like what does sonification offer?

01:02:39.860 --> 01:02:41.660
And it's also, it does have this side

01:02:41.660 --> 01:02:45.040
of psychological dimension too, which is,

01:02:45.040 --> 01:02:47.893
you know, I think it
has a lot of potential.

01:02:47.893 --> 01:02:50.310
- Indeed, okay, we have many questions.

01:02:50.310 --> 01:02:52.213
It's a measure of how
engaged the audience is

01:02:52.213 --> 01:02:54.470
with your wonderful presentation.

01:02:54.470 --> 01:02:57.900
Beth asks, "What, if any,
impact do the sounds made

01:02:57.900 --> 01:03:02.030
by the animals have when
sonifying their movement?

01:03:02.030 --> 01:03:04.980
Do you ever integrate their natural sounds

01:03:04.980 --> 01:03:09.147
with the instrumental sounds,
and if so, is it harmonic?"

01:03:10.642 --> 01:03:15.642
- (laughs) We have started
to experiment with that,

01:03:16.000 --> 01:03:20.070
and so we have these wonderful,

01:03:20.070 --> 01:03:23.210
I had an AudioMoth in my backyard

01:03:23.210 --> 01:03:24.860
just sitting there for a month,

01:03:24.860 --> 01:03:28.720
and yes, it records every 20 minutes,

01:03:28.720 --> 01:03:32.580
and we have like
absolutely enormous amounts

01:03:32.580 --> 01:03:36.380
of these recordings, and I
mean, it really does work

01:03:36.380 --> 01:03:39.720
just to play the recording using, say,

01:03:39.720 --> 01:03:43.220
the Merlin app from the
Cornell Ornithology Lab,

01:03:43.220 --> 01:03:44.583
and it will identify it.

01:03:45.520 --> 01:03:50.080
So the idea is to create
ambient backgrounds

01:03:50.080 --> 01:03:55.020
that are essentially filtered,
you know, filtering out I-95,

01:03:55.020 --> 01:03:57.333
which is also not too
far away from my house.

01:03:58.350 --> 01:04:01.440
And you can create rather wonderful

01:04:01.440 --> 01:04:06.374
shifting musical textures,

01:04:06.374 --> 01:04:08.040
and the idea is that we would put those

01:04:08.040 --> 01:04:13.040
behind the more frenetic tracking data.

01:04:14.310 --> 01:04:17.630
So I'm really glad that
someone had that observation.

01:04:17.630 --> 01:04:20.620
That's a, yeah, it's good thought.

01:04:20.620 --> 01:04:23.590
- Yeah, as Matthew said,
that's a great point.

01:04:23.590 --> 01:04:27.240
And yeah, I'm working on a
song called "Glitch Crab"

01:04:27.240 --> 01:04:29.400
where I wanna map some melodies

01:04:29.400 --> 01:04:31.480
made by craps using computer vision,

01:04:31.480 --> 01:04:34.800
but also the sound they
make when they close their,

01:04:34.800 --> 01:04:36.830
whatever this is called, so stay tuned.

01:04:36.830 --> 01:04:39.460
It will come to my SoundCloud. (laughs)

01:04:39.460 --> 01:04:42.780
- Yeah, and please share
it with us. We'll post it.

01:04:42.780 --> 01:04:45.117
Okay, an evocative question from Steven,

01:04:45.117 --> 01:04:48.340
"Some giant tortoises still alive today

01:04:48.340 --> 01:04:52.250
were also alive when Beethoven went deaf.

01:04:52.250 --> 01:04:53.670
How do we tell the story

01:04:53.670 --> 01:04:57.770
of a 200-year-old life through music?

01:04:57.770 --> 01:04:59.510
Can we bring the living tortoise

01:04:59.510 --> 01:05:01.377
and dead Beethoven together?"

01:05:04.300 --> 01:05:06.470
- I think Matthew might
answer this question

01:05:06.470 --> 01:05:10.783
as he has been working on an
opera with people who were.

01:05:11.640 --> 01:05:15.820
- Oh, yes, so I'm working on
an opera about Alan Turing,

01:05:15.820 --> 01:05:19.810
and we've trained it, the
libretto was written by an AI.

01:05:19.810 --> 01:05:23.430
We're using GPT-2 and
GPT-3, and we've trained it

01:05:23.430 --> 01:05:27.000
only on Turing's published papers

01:05:27.000 --> 01:05:30.750
and also books that we knew
that he had read as a child.

01:05:30.750 --> 01:05:32.377
And then you ask the AI,

01:05:32.377 --> 01:05:34.100
"Can computers think? Can machines think?"

01:05:34.100 --> 01:05:38.540
And you get these rather
extraordinary answers.

01:05:38.540 --> 01:05:43.050
So I think there's possibly potential

01:05:43.050 --> 01:05:45.440
to use artificial intelligence

01:05:45.440 --> 01:05:49.950
for predictive sound-making,

01:05:49.950 --> 01:05:52.620
and we have done some
experiments with that.

01:05:52.620 --> 01:05:54.490
You can use this, these same algorithms

01:05:54.490 --> 01:05:56.430
can be used for images as well.

01:05:56.430 --> 01:06:01.020
So it's not, GPT-2 or GPT-3
are a natural language model.

01:06:01.020 --> 01:06:05.010
So primarily they're there
for language construction,

01:06:05.010 --> 01:06:10.010
but you can use them for
sound and image as well.

01:06:10.290 --> 01:06:15.290
So yeah, so that's kind of a
Holy Grail, but sure. (laughs)

01:06:18.111 --> 01:06:20.030
- Something to aspire to.

01:06:20.030 --> 01:06:20.863
- Yeah.
- From Richard,

01:06:20.863 --> 01:06:24.430
"What about tracking radiant
shifts in internal parameters,

01:06:24.430 --> 01:06:28.317
such as temperature, electrical
activity, et cetera?"

01:06:29.890 --> 01:06:33.340
- That's a great idea,
and one of my PhD chapters

01:06:33.340 --> 01:06:37.060
is actually to see whether
animals can predict weather.

01:06:37.060 --> 01:06:40.790
And I think anyone who
has seen bird flying

01:06:40.790 --> 01:06:44.693
knows where thermals are, so
that's a really good idea.

01:06:45.880 --> 01:06:48.160
We have increasingly a good ability

01:06:48.160 --> 01:06:50.340
to track the physiology
of animals in the wild.

01:06:50.340 --> 01:06:53.900
And we have mechanistic
models that are based

01:06:53.900 --> 01:06:57.690
on energy expenditure of
animals that allow us to,

01:06:57.690 --> 01:06:59.510
we could build on these theories

01:06:59.510 --> 01:07:00.760
and create these principles,

01:07:00.760 --> 01:07:02.910
just how we have created the principles

01:07:02.910 --> 01:07:07.520
of collective behavior and
translate those to physiology.

01:07:07.520 --> 01:07:10.610
I'm kinda improvising here,
but if a mice eats something,

01:07:10.610 --> 01:07:13.160
you could look at its heart rate,

01:07:13.160 --> 01:07:14.980
or you could look at its caloric intake

01:07:14.980 --> 01:07:18.140
and play around with that,
and maybe the sound of cake

01:07:18.140 --> 01:07:19.820
eaten by a mice sounds
different than a carrot.

01:07:19.820 --> 01:07:22.390
I don't know. (laughs)

01:07:22.390 --> 01:07:24.460
- That was Richard Prum's
suggestion, actually,

01:07:24.460 --> 01:07:28.730
when he came to visit, when
he visited the exhibition

01:07:28.730 --> 01:07:31.150
and the performance at Tsai CITY,

01:07:31.150 --> 01:07:36.150
and he thought that that
would be a way of tracking,

01:07:37.060 --> 01:07:39.834
say, certainly like starlings and things

01:07:39.834 --> 01:07:42.830
and these sort of mass demonstrations

01:07:42.830 --> 01:07:46.233
that you see in nature, so
that's a great suggestion.

01:07:50.150 --> 01:07:53.063
- Great, one more question
before we wrap up.

01:07:54.530 --> 01:07:57.497
Let's see, it says, the participant asks,

01:07:57.497 --> 01:07:59.730
"I was wondering if you have
thought about how to interact

01:07:59.730 --> 01:08:03.910
with Indigenous non-scientific,
non-Western interpretations

01:08:03.910 --> 01:08:06.210
of animal movements and sounds?

01:08:06.210 --> 01:08:07.490
Indigenous groups throughout the world

01:08:07.490 --> 01:08:09.330
have local alternative ways

01:08:09.330 --> 01:08:11.890
of interpreting animal
sounds and movements.

01:08:11.890 --> 01:08:13.800
For example, the songs of certain birds

01:08:13.800 --> 01:08:16.880
mean certain things for
particular Indigenous groups,

01:08:16.880 --> 01:08:18.600
and certain communities have a history

01:08:18.600 --> 01:08:20.773
of imitating and
incorporating animal sounds

01:08:20.773 --> 01:08:24.370
into their own musical practices
and movement practices.

01:08:24.370 --> 01:08:28.260
So how have these modes of
listening and mapping sound,

01:08:28.260 --> 01:08:30.750
how could they be put in conversation

01:08:30.750 --> 01:08:32.090
with your own projects?"

01:08:32.090 --> 01:08:33.227
Very insightful question.

01:08:33.227 --> 01:08:34.530
- Oh, I love that question.

01:08:34.530 --> 01:08:36.070
Thank you so much for bringing that up.

01:08:36.070 --> 01:08:38.300
And as someone who's
from the Global South,

01:08:38.300 --> 01:08:40.740
this is incredibly
important to me personally

01:08:40.740 --> 01:08:43.663
on every single level,
including my science.

01:08:45.030 --> 01:08:48.180
And as an example, the
country I'm from, Uruguay,

01:08:48.180 --> 01:08:51.573
actually means river of the painted birds.

01:08:52.490 --> 01:08:55.770
Gua means coming off, y means water,

01:08:55.770 --> 01:08:59.120
and uru could mean zone
of or a type of bird.

01:08:59.120 --> 01:09:02.440
So everything in our
language, our reality,

01:09:02.440 --> 01:09:05.970
names of places have a
variety of different meaning

01:09:05.970 --> 01:09:07.820
depending on different culture,

01:09:07.820 --> 01:09:09.650
different spiritual practices.

01:09:09.650 --> 01:09:14.250
So I'm incredibly excited of
when I go to home this year

01:09:14.250 --> 01:09:16.510
in South America and
travel a little bit more,

01:09:16.510 --> 01:09:18.800
it's been hard with the COVID-19 pandemic,

01:09:18.800 --> 01:09:22.950
to learn more and listen
how different animals

01:09:22.950 --> 01:09:25.090
have been perceived through culture,

01:09:25.090 --> 01:09:27.573
especially as we start thinking about,

01:09:28.820 --> 01:09:30.940
for example, United
States Land Back movements

01:09:30.940 --> 01:09:33.990
and how this relates to the
cultural heritage of people.

01:09:33.990 --> 01:09:35.840
This is a great question.

01:09:35.840 --> 01:09:36.930
- No, it's so fantastic question.

01:09:36.930 --> 01:09:41.720
So I composed an opera
a number of years ago

01:09:41.720 --> 01:09:45.700
about the meeting of the Europeans

01:09:45.700 --> 01:09:47.690
with Maori in New Zealand,

01:09:47.690 --> 01:09:50.100
so when Captain Cook first came,

01:09:50.100 --> 01:09:53.100
and I collaborated with a Maori musician

01:09:53.100 --> 01:09:55.680
who was playing a variety of instruments,

01:09:55.680 --> 01:09:57.930
and she was a trained musician,

01:09:57.930 --> 01:09:59.970
but it wasn't a useful conversation

01:09:59.970 --> 01:10:03.530
to sort of approach it
from a Western perspective.

01:10:03.530 --> 01:10:05.750
So the most valuable thing to do

01:10:05.750 --> 01:10:10.750
is to talk about the story
and the place and the material

01:10:10.820 --> 01:10:14.330
and the purpose of the
instruments that she was using.

01:10:14.330 --> 01:10:15.700
And then through that essentially

01:10:15.700 --> 01:10:18.140
kind of dramaturgical conversation,

01:10:18.140 --> 01:10:22.540
we were able to find a
place for that in the music.

01:10:22.540 --> 01:10:25.250
So I think that the most
important step, of course,

01:10:25.250 --> 01:10:27.160
is the nature of the conversation

01:10:27.160 --> 01:10:29.470
and figuring out how to communicate.

01:10:29.470 --> 01:10:32.040
So it's a wonderful
question, and, you know,

01:10:32.040 --> 01:10:36.030
I would absolutely want
to investigate that.

01:10:36.030 --> 01:10:38.900
And the Maori name for
New Zealand, Aotearoa,

01:10:38.900 --> 01:10:40.500
means land of the long white cloud,

01:10:40.500 --> 01:10:45.447
so we can talk about origin
names (laughs), yeah.

01:10:48.570 --> 01:10:53.570
- Anyway, let me take this moment

01:10:53.750 --> 01:10:58.300
to once again thank you
both, Diego and Matthew,

01:10:58.300 --> 01:11:00.340
for an absolutely wonderful journey.

01:11:00.340 --> 01:11:03.280
What an exciting, inspiring project.

01:11:03.280 --> 01:11:08.280
And I think that the prospect
of conveying the urgency

01:11:08.710 --> 01:11:12.760
of climate change, the
loss in biodiversity,

01:11:12.760 --> 01:11:17.230
if sonification can
emotionally compel people

01:11:17.230 --> 01:11:21.330
to believing it and
springing into action, gosh,

01:11:21.330 --> 01:11:23.170
we would be living in
a very different world.

01:11:23.170 --> 01:11:27.350
So collectively with sort of, I mean,

01:11:27.350 --> 01:11:29.080
this was absolutely thrilling.

01:11:29.080 --> 01:11:30.250
We wish you very well.

01:11:30.250 --> 01:11:32.870
Please keep us posted with the work.

01:11:32.870 --> 01:11:37.870
And thank you so much, Ty,
for helping set up the event

01:11:38.490 --> 01:11:43.490
and to Guy for recording
and making this all happen.

01:11:44.910 --> 01:11:49.540
And Ty, also, thanks for handling
these wonderful questions.

01:11:49.540 --> 01:11:52.700
Thanks to the audience
for engaging so deeply

01:11:52.700 --> 01:11:55.460
and asking such meaningful questions.

01:11:55.460 --> 01:11:58.860
So before I draw the event to a close,

01:11:58.860 --> 01:12:01.880
I just want to mention that we have

01:12:01.880 --> 01:12:05.820
one other Franke Fellow
to close out this season

01:12:05.820 --> 01:12:08.400
this year, this academic year.

01:12:08.400 --> 01:12:11.553
Dr. Ana Florence, a former
a Franke Fellow as well,

01:12:12.440 --> 01:12:16.700
who has moved, has gotten a
faculty position at Columbia.

01:12:16.700 --> 01:12:17.960
And she'll be talking to us

01:12:17.960 --> 01:12:20.730
about "Social and Structural Contours

01:12:20.730 --> 01:12:24.460
of Marginalization in
the Context of COVID-19,"

01:12:24.460 --> 01:12:27.750
talking about her work
with marginalized groups.

01:12:27.750 --> 01:12:32.750
So once again, thank you
very much, and bye, everyone.

01:12:33.640 --> 01:12:36.950
And Matthew and Diego, this project

01:12:36.950 --> 01:12:39.290
is just absolutely thrilling.

01:12:39.290 --> 01:12:41.050
I hope you get a chance to share it

01:12:41.050 --> 01:12:42.770
with many, many more people.

01:12:42.770 --> 01:12:47.410
We'd be happy to advertise what
you're doing on our website.

01:12:47.410 --> 01:12:49.100
And Matthew, please do invite us

01:12:49.100 --> 01:12:51.250
to your opera when it's done.

01:12:51.250 --> 01:12:52.083
- Oh, sure. (laughs)

01:12:52.083 --> 01:12:55.410
- Yeah, and I think what is
very exciting for me as well

01:12:55.410 --> 01:12:59.800
was a recent Nobel Prize in
physics went to Giorgio Parisi,

01:12:59.800 --> 01:13:02.700
who actually worked out
the collective phenomena,

01:13:02.700 --> 01:13:05.710
the description, the language to describe

01:13:05.710 --> 01:13:08.253
the motion of starlings, for example,

01:13:09.753 --> 01:13:12.450
which is the same kind of
formalism that you would have

01:13:12.450 --> 01:13:15.720
to describe the collective
behavior of atoms.

01:13:15.720 --> 01:13:20.150
So it's the same underlying
framework that transcends.

01:13:20.150 --> 01:13:23.140
So your work is inspiring very similarly

01:13:23.140 --> 01:13:25.583
with the kinds of applications, thank you.

